{
"slug": "padel-shot-selection",
"cat": "Tactics",
"title": "Padel Shot Selection: The Cheat Sheet for Every Position",
"h1": "Padel shot selection cheat sheet",
"desc": "Padel shot selection made simple: tables of the best shot for each situation, at the back, at the net, off the back glass, off the side glass and in transition.",
"lede": "Good padel shot selection comes down to one question: what is this ball, from this position, allowed to do? From the back you mostly lob and reset, in the middle you build, and at the net you attack the feet, the middle and the angles. The tables below give you the percentage choice for every common situation, so you can make the decision before the ball arrives.",
"books": ["beginner", "advanced"],
"board": None,
"date": "2026-11-08",
"sources": [BABOLAT, BOUNCE, TPS_BACKGLASS, TPS_DEAL_LOBS, FIP],
"body": """
<h2>Padel shot selection: three rules behind every choice</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Height decides attack or defence.</strong> The Padel School's rule of thumb: a ball between your ankles and knees is for defending with a soft ball, waist to chest allows a controlled shot with some aggression, and above the shoulders is for attacking.</li>
<li><strong>Position decides how much risk you can take.</strong> Babolat's model splits the court into three zones: the back (last two metres before the glass) where you stay in the rally, the middle where you build, and the net where you finish.</li>
<li><strong>Height before speed.</strong> Bounce's strategy guide sums up percentage padel in those three words. When in doubt, a high deep ball beats a fast flat one.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Both of you at the back, opponents at the net</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Best shot</th><th>Avoid</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Opponents tight to the net</td><td>Deep lob, ideally cross-court</td><td>Drives at their racket</td></tr>
<tr><td>Opponents around the service line</td><td><a href="/padel-chiquita">Chiquita</a> at their feet</td><td>A lob they can step back to</td></tr>
<tr><td>Comfortable, waist-high ball</td><td>Lob, or firm low ball through the middle</td><td>Trying to hit a winner</td></tr>
<tr><td>You're rushed or off balance</td><td>High lob to buy time</td><td>Anything flat</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ball low at your feet</td><td>Soft, high reset to the middle</td><td>Attacking up from below the net</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The lob direction rule from our <a href="/padel-lob">lob guide</a>: cross-court has the most margin, down the line is hard to smash but has the least margin, and a high lob down the middle is the easiest to attack.</p>

<h2>Both of you at the net</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Best shot</th><th>Avoid</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>High, comfortable volley</td><td>Volley to the feet, the middle or a sharp angle</td><td>Volleying back to the player at the glass</td></tr>
<tr><td>Low ball at your feet</td><td>Soft, deep volley; keep your position</td><td>Attacking from below the net</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lob over you, mid-depth</td><td><a href="/how-to-hit-a-bandeja">Bandeja</a> deep, recover forward</td><td>A big smash that comes back off their glass</td></tr>
<tr><td>Short, slow, high lob</td><td>Smash or <a href="/padel-vibora">víbora</a> to finish</td><td>A cautious bandeja that lets them back in</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deep lob over your head</td><td>Turn, let it bounce, play after the back glass</td><td>Jumping at it off balance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hard drive at your body</td><td>Block volley into space</td><td>A full swing</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The Padel School's lob breakdown fits this table: short lobs get the víbora or attacking overhead, central lobs the bandeja or víbora, deep lobs go off the back glass.</p>

<h2>Ball off the back glass</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Rebound</th><th>Best shot</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Low rebound</td><td>Deep cross-court ball</td><td>Most margin, keeps you neutral</td></tr>
<tr><td>High rebound</td><td>Lob</td><td>Pushes the net pair back</td></tr>
<tr><td>High, sitting rebound with opponents back</td><td><a href="/padel-bajada">Bajada</a> (overhead off the glass)</td><td>A rare chance to attack from the back</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ball stuck behind your hip</td><td>Safe, high reset</td><td>You can't control it, so don't force it</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The first, second and last rows follow Bounce's back-glass advice: attack only when the ball comes far enough forward to hit in front of your body. Start your swing as the ball is about to hit the glass, as The Padel School recommends. Remember the rules are on your side here: you may play the ball off your own glass, but never off your own mesh (FIP Rules 14b and 13l).</p>

<h2>Ball off the side glass</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Best shot</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Ball coming out of the side glass at a good height</td><td>Lob or deep cross-court</td><td>Rushing before it comes out</td></tr>
<tr><td>Low, dying ball near the side glass</td><td>Play it before the wall, soft and high</td><td>Waiting for a bounce that won't come up</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ball into the corner (back then side, or side then back)</td><td>Let it come out, then lob</td><td>Standing in the corner where it ends up</td></tr>
<tr><td>You're close to the side glass at the net</td><td>Safe volley to the middle</td><td>Big angles that need perfect timing</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Babolat's rule: the closer you are to a side wall, the more cautiously you should play. Our guide to <a href="/padel-side-wall">the padel side wall</a> covers double-wall rebounds.</p>

<h2>In the middle of the court (transition)</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Ball</th><th>Best shot</th><th>Next move</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Waist-high, comfortable</td><td>Accelerate cross-court or down the middle</td><td>Move forward together</td></tr>
<tr><td>Low</td><td>Patient ball to the opponents' feet</td><td>Follow it in if they volley up</td></tr>
<tr><td>Difficult or rushed</td><td>High, deep ball to reset</td><td>Drop back as a pair</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>All three rows come straight from Babolat's middle-zone guidance. The middle of the court is a place to pass through, not to stay, as our <a href="/padel-positioning-doubles">positioning guide</a> explains.</p>

<h2>Serve and return</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Percentage choice</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Your serve</td><td>Make the receiver contact the ball low or late, then follow it to the net</td></tr>
<tr><td>Return, server rushing in</td><td>Low at the server's feet</td></tr>
<tr><td>Return, default</td><td>Deep through the middle rather than a sharp angle</td></tr>
<tr><td>Return against an aggressive net player</td><td>Lob</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>How to use this cheat sheet</h2>
<p>Don't try to memorise every row. Pick the one table that matches where you lose most points, play a match with that table in mind, and add another next week. Before each shot, ask two quick questions: where am I, and how high is the ball? Those two answers point you to the right row. If you're still unsure when the ball arrives, the default is always the same: high, deep and through the middle or cross-court.</p>
<h2>Percentage padel in one paragraph</h2>
<p>Most club points are lost, not won. Pick the shot with the most margin for the ball you've been given: higher over the net, more central, deeper. Attack only balls above the shoulder or sitting up at waist height near the net. Save the risky shots for when you're in balance and in the right zone. Bounce lists trying to win from the defensive zone and smashing every overhead among the commonest mistakes; avoiding those two alone will win you points.</p>
<p>The <a href="/the-padel-playbook-beginner-intermediate">Padel Playbook: Beginner &amp; Intermediate</a> explains positions and the walls with court diagrams, and <a href="/the-padel-playbook-intermediate-advanced">The Padel Playbook: Intermediate &amp; Advanced</a> takes shot choice further into net control and match tactics.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<div class="faq">
<details><summary>What is the most important shot in padel?</summary><p>The lob. It's the main way the defending pair wins back the net, and it's the safest choice whenever you're at the back and under pressure.</p></details>
<details><summary>When should I attack in padel?</summary><p>When the ball is above your shoulder, or sitting up at waist height near the net, and you're in balance. Low balls are for defending with a soft, controlled shot.</p></details>
<details><summary>Where should I hit the ball in padel?</summary><p>From the back, deep and usually cross-court or high through the middle. At the net, at the opponents' feet, through the middle between them, or to an angle that makes the next ball float.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should I smash or play a bandeja?</summary><p>Play the bandeja on most lobs and recover to the net. Smash only when the ball is high, slow and clearly finishable, because a smash that comes back off the glass often costs you the net.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can I hit the ball into my own side glass?</summary><p>Yes. Playing the ball against your own glass so it then crosses the net is legal (FIP Rule 14b). Hitting your own mesh fence is not (Rule 13l).</p></details>
</div>
""",
},
