{
"slug": "how-to-turn-a-padel-match-around",
"cat": "Tactics",
"title": "How to Come Back in Padel: A Checklist for Losing Matches",
"h1": "How to come back in padel: the turnaround checklist",
"desc": "How to come back in padel when you're losing: a checklist to diagnose the problem, change pace, lob more, target the right player and use every legal break.",
"lede": "To come back in padel, first work out whether you're losing to their good play or your own errors, then change one thing at a time: slow the ball down, lob more, target the weaker opponent and keep it simple on the big points. Panic and random winners rarely turn a match. A short plan, used at every change of ends, often does. Print this checklist or keep it on your phone for your next match.",
"books": ["advanced", "drill2"],
"board": None,
"date": "2026-11-08",
"sources": [AFP_SLUMP, PB_STRONGER, FIP, TPS_SLOW],
"body": """
<h2>How to come back in padel: diagnose first</h2>
<p>All For Padel's coaching advice on bad patches starts with a question: is the problem the opponents, or your own mistakes? The fix is different for each, so answer it honestly at the next change of ends.</p>
<ul>
<li>Are we losing points to their winners, or to our unforced errors?</li>
<li>Is one of us struggling more than the other?</li>
<li>Are they at the net most of the time?</li>
<li>Is one opponent clearly doing the damage?</li>
<li>Are we rushing between points?</li>
</ul>
<p>Two or three honest answers are enough to choose a plan below.</p>

<h2>If your errors are the problem</h2>
<p>All For Padel says most unforced errors come from poor preparation or positioning before the shot, not from the contact, and names relaxing too much and lack of movement as the commonest causes.</p>
<ul>
<li>Move your feet on every ball, including the easy ones.</li>
<li>Prepare the racket earlier, especially off the <a href="/padel-back-glass">back glass</a>.</li>
<li>Aim bigger: higher over the net, further inside the lines, more through the middle.</li>
<li>Pick one simple pattern, such as playing everything to one opponent, to give your mind a job. All For Padel suggests exactly this to recover concentration.</li>
<li>If only one of you is struggling, play more cross-court balls to the opponent in front of you, so your partner gets fewer balls and time to recover.</li>
</ul>

<h2>If the opponents are simply playing better</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Change the pace.</strong> Padel Browser's advice against stronger pairs is to stop matching their pace: higher, deeper, slower balls with more margin, so they have to create the speed. The Padel School recommends slow balls as a way to break a hard hitter's rhythm.</li>
<li><strong>Lob more, and lob deep.</strong> All For Padel recommends high lobs to unsettle opponents. A deep <a href="/padel-lob">lob</a> is also how you take the net away from them.</li>
<li><strong>Use the conditions.</strong> Outdoors, lob into the sun or with the wind, as All For Padel suggests. See <a href="/playing-padel-outdoors-wind-sun">playing padel in wind and sun</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Slow down the start of each point.</strong> All For Padel's tip for both serving and receiving. A more careful serve and a controlled return take away their easy first attack.</li>
<li><strong>Target the weaker opponent.</strong> Padel Browser suggests sending about 70% of the ball to the weaker player rather than all of it, and giving that plan a full set before abandoning it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Use every second the rules give you</h2>
<p>You're allowed time to think. Use it, but don't abuse it: the FIP rules set the limits.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Break</th><th>Maximum (FIP Rule 2)</th><th>What to do with it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Between points</td><td>20 seconds</td><td>Turn away, breathe, agree the next serve or return target in a few words</td></tr>
<tr><td>Change of ends</td><td>90 seconds</td><td>Drink, then one diagnosis and one change</td></tr>
<tr><td>After the first game of a set</td><td>No rest: play is continuous</td><td>Swap ends and get straight on with it</td></tr>
<tr><td>End of a set</td><td>120 seconds</td><td>Choose the plan, and the sides, for the next set</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>You change ends after the first, third and every following odd game of a set (FIP Rule 5); our <a href="/padel-change-of-ends">change of ends guide</a> has the details. Remember too that the server must wait until the receiver is ready, so as the receiving pair you can take a moment to settle, within reason.</p>

<h2>Can you switch sides to turn it around?</h2>
<p>Not in the middle of a set, if you mean who receives on which side. FIP Rule 8.2 says the receiving pair decides who receives first in the first game of each set, and that player receives the first serve of every game in the set; Rule 8.3 keeps the receiving order fixed for the set. The serving order is also set before each set (Rule 6.8). So swapping who plays the right and left as receiver can only happen at the start of a new set.</p>
<p>What you can change within a set:</p>
<ul>
<li>Your serving formation, for example switching to the <a href="/padel-australian-formation">Australian formation</a>, since the server's partner may stand anywhere (Rule 3).</li>
<li>Who covers the middle and the lobs during points.</li>
<li>Where you stand to receive: the receiver's position isn't fixed by the rules, as long as the serve bounces before you play it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Simple comeback plans</h2>
<p>Pick one. Run it for at least three games before judging it.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Use it when</th><th>What you do</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Slow and deep</td><td>They're out-hitting you</td><td>High, deep balls and lobs only; no drives from the back</td></tr>
<tr><td>Take the net</td><td>They're camped at the net</td><td>Lob deep cross-court, follow it in together, bandeja to stay</td></tr>
<tr><td>One target</td><td>One opponent is clearly weaker</td><td>Most balls to that player, including lobs and returns</td></tr>
<tr><td>Margin first</td><td>Your errors are the problem</td><td>Middle of the court, middle of the net, no winners from the back</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>The big points checklist</h2>
<p>Comebacks are made on a few points: break points, 40-40 and the first points of a new set. Padel Browser suggests playing for the break rather than winners, and owning the deciding point with the safe version: first serve in, deep central return, no heroics. With <a href="/golden-point-padel">golden point</a> scoring, that single deuce point decides the game, so have your plan ready before you reach it.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li>Agree the serve or return target before you step up.</li>
<li>First serve in, with placement rather than pace.</li>
<li>Return deep through the middle.</li>
<li>Lob if they rush the net.</li>
<li>Let them hit the risky shot.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Keep your heads</h2>
<p>Padel Browser's last piece of advice is easy to forget: at changes of ends give one positive, forward-looking instruction, not a post-mortem. Our guide to <a href="/padel-mental-game">the padel mental game</a> goes deeper into staying calm under pressure, and <a href="/the-padel-playbook-intermediate-advanced">The Padel Playbook: Intermediate &amp; Advanced</a> covers match tactics in detail. To rehearse pressure situations in training, the pressure games in the <a href="/the-padel-drill-book-intermediate-advanced">Padel Drill Book: Intermediate &amp; Advanced</a> are a good start.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<div class="faq">
<details><summary>How do you come back from losing in padel?</summary><p>Work out whether the problem is their good play or your errors, then change one thing: slow the pace, lob more, target the weaker opponent or simply aim with more margin. Give the new plan a few games before you judge it.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can you change sides in the middle of a padel set?</summary><p>Not as receivers: the receiving order is fixed for the whole set (FIP Rules 8.2 and 8.3). You can change it at the start of the next set, and you can change serving formation or positions during points at any time.</p></details>
<details><summary>How long can you take between points in padel?</summary><p>Up to 20 seconds under FIP Rule 2. Changes of ends allow up to 90 seconds, and the break at the end of a set up to 120 seconds.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should I hit harder when I'm losing?</summary><p>Usually not. Hitting harder adds errors, and the walls bring hard shots back. Slower, higher and deeper balls tend to work better against a pair who are beating you.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is it fair to target the weaker player when losing?</summary><p>Yes. It's a normal tactic at every level. Coaches suggest sending most, not all, of the ball to the weaker player so the opponents can't simply adjust.</p></details>
</div>
""",
},
